/* Local Operator TUI — island-dark, borderless, one green.
 *
 * Brand tokens ($lo-*) are injected by OperatorApp.get_css_variables() from
 * local_operator.tui.theme (single source of truth; this file never carries
 * a literal hex). The dark ramp is the product's "island" night.
 *
 * Minimalism contract (density is the brand):
 *   - NO bordered boxes anywhere. Structure comes from symbols, tint, and
 *     spacing. The app draws exactly ONE persistent structural rule, and it is
 *     not an edge of anything: the user prompt's $lo-dim gutter bar, which
 *     runs down the left of every row of a message so a multi-paragraph prompt
 *     reads as ONE block. (This used to say TWO and name "the input panel's
 *     thin top line" as the other. That rule does not exist — it was removed
 *     deliberately, see the `#input-shell` comment; there is no `border-top`,
 *     no `Rule(` and no `─` in any input widget. Focus still brightens the
 *     chevron and never a rule, D23/D24.) Overlay cards may draw ONE $lo-faint
 *     horizontal directly under their own title and nowhere else, which is
 *     what every `─` in the app is. A rule that closes a shape is a box; a
 *     rule that marks a margin is a gutter; a rule under a title is a caption.
 *   - NO uniform filler rows. Blocks own no outer margin; separation is
 *     ADAPTIVE and rides one opt-in class (.gap-above) that the transcript
 *     container applies only where the rhythm needs it — a run of one-line
 *     notices stays flush, while prose, expanded output and each separate
 *     tool action get a blank row (SPACING_AIRY in widgets/transcript.py).
 *   - one line per tool action while collapsed; each action is a subtle
 *     background-filled card (elevation = a background step, never a border
 *     or shadow) whose FILL carries the outcome
 *   - the accent green means ONE thing: THIS is what the turn is on — the work
 *     in flight while the agent holds the turn, and the answer the app acts on
 *     the moment the user takes it back. (It used to say "a turn is live",
 *     which was not true of site 4 and so was not true of the frames: a picker
 *     highlight is not liveness, it is the other half of the same sentence.)
 *     It is spent on exactly five sites, and the list is exhaustive —
 *     `grep -rn 'accent' local_operator/tui`
 *     plus `$lo-accent` in this file should find no others. Grep the WORD, not
 *     `semantic_color("accent")`: shimmer.py reaches it through its own
 *     `_style("accent")` helper and command_picker.py through
 *     `semantic_color("accent" if selected else "fg")`, so the narrower pattern
 *     silently misses two of the five and reads as a clean audit.
 *       1. the working line's shimmer crest      (shimmer.py)
 *       2. a running tool's glyph                (widgets/tool_card.py)
 *       3. the status band's streaming spinner   (widgets/status_line.py)
 *       4. what ENTER WILL TAKE in a picker      (widgets/command_picker.py,
 *          widgets/model_picker.py, widgets/ask_picker.py). ONE site, not
 *          three, and stated as what Enter takes rather than as "the
 *          highlighted row", because on a multi-select those are two different
 *          rows: the ask picker paints the ticked `[x]` boxes AND their labels,
 *          and leaves the label under the cursor alone when its box is empty,
 *          because Enter would not take it. Painted the other way round the ink
 *          made two claims in one frame — `[x]` on row 1 (chosen) and the label
 *          of row 3 (merely under the cursor) — and this list was not what the
 *          frame said (D11, round 1). The cursor is carried by `tint-select`
 *          and the caret, which is what those are for. The ask picker's label
 *          was the violet `label` token first, which read as another product's
 *          theme; its caret is `muted` for the reason site 4 already gives in
 *          command_picker.py — a second green glyph beside a green label reads
 *          as a duplicated caret. `/settings` is part of THIS site, not a
 *          fifth: its row cursor `›`, its enum radio `●`, its edit caret `▏`
 *          and its live pane tab are all "what Enter will take", stated in the
 *          same sentence as the pickers. Named explicitly because the page
 *          paints them in three different row kinds at once, and a reader
 *          auditing the frame would otherwise count three new accent sites
 *          against a list claiming to be exhaustive (design round 1, D5).
 *       5. a markdown h1                        (markdown_theme.py)
 *          The one site that is NOT liveness, and it is admitted knowingly.
 *          h1, h2 and strong all painted `fg` bold — the same pixels — so a
 *          long answer had no top-level anchor, and the neutral ramp had no
 *          rung left to buy one with (h2-h6 already spend three). Structure
 *          at the top of a document is worth one hue; h2-h6 stay neutral and
 *          this does not generalise to a heading palette. Cheapest site to
 *          give back if it reads badly — `label` is the alternative.
 *     It was FIVE, then FOUR when the focused input chevron gave its site
 *     back — focus is not liveness, and painting both green put a green
 *     chevron two rows above the band's green spinner meaning something else
 *     (D5, round 11). Focus is a brightness step in the neutral ramp now —
 *     see `#prompt-chevron`. It is FIVE again because h1 was added on the
 *     budget written up in `docs/proposals/theme-colour-budget.md`; that is a
 *     budget decision, deliberately taken, not a site that crept in.
 *     Everything else is warm neutrals. Links and file paths are the `signal`
 *     blue and deliberately NOT green; `/usage` provider headings are `label`;
 *     the brand glyph in the band is `muted`, because a mark that is always on
 *     screen cannot also mean "running" — see theme.py. Before adding a sixth,
 *     take one away: an accent spent everywhere is an accent spent nowhere.
 *
 * Edge spacing: one cell at the left, right, and bottom screen edges; NONE
 * along the top while scrolling (content meets the top edge). The transcript
 * also ends in one row of ground, so the conversation never touches the
 * composer — see `TranscriptView` for why that row is the transcript's.
 */

#session-workspace {
    width: 1fr;
    height: 1fr;
    layers: default sidebar;
}

#session-conversation {
    width: 1fr;
    height: 1fr;
}

/* Width and horizontal padding are set by `_sync_sidebar_layout`, not here:
 * the gutter that separates the list from the conversation has to MIRROR with
 * `tui.sidebar_position` (right edge when docked left, left edge when docked
 * right), which a static rule cannot express. The values below are the
 * left-docked resting state so the widget still renders sanely before the
 * first layout pass. */
SessionSidebar {
    width: 33;
    height: 1fr;
    padding: 0 3 0 1;
    background: $lo-bg;
    color: $lo-fg;
    overflow: hidden;
    /* Every row is a click target that attaches that session, so the pointer
     * says so before the click does — the same affordance `SubagentRow` and
     * the subagent-view hints carry. Textual emits this as an OSC pointer
     * shape; a terminal without the Kitty pointer-shape protocol ignores it
     * and loses nothing, which is why the row's hover tint (painted in
     * `render`, since rows are Text spans in ONE widget and cannot be reached
     * by `:hover`) is the affordance that does not depend on it. */
    pointer: pointer;
}

/* Small terminals keep the conversation's full line length. Each layer lays
 * out independently, so the list overlays only the transcript; its height is
 * bounded above the live input dock by the app. No frame or dividing rule. */
.sidebar-overlay SessionSidebar {
    layer: sidebar;
    background: $lo-surface;
}

Screen {
    background: $lo-bg;
    color: $lo-fg;
    /* Outer inset for the WHOLE app, matching the omp reference: the chrome is
     * borderless, so the breathing room at the terminal's edges is what makes
     * it read as a deliberate surface rather than text that ran out of room.
     * One row top and bottom, one cell left and right. Children then only
     * manage spacing BETWEEN themselves. */
    padding: 1;
    /* A second layer for the toast overlay. Layers are arranged independently
     * over the whole region, which is the property that matters: without one,
     * a toast at the top of the screen would STEAL a row from the transcript
     * and reflow the conversation for five seconds. It stops the LAYOUT
     * reflowing — it does NOT stop the screen being erased, so the host is
     * sized to its card (see `#toast-host`) and not to the width. Everything
     * else stays on `default` (Textual's name for the unset layer), so this
     * declaration changes no existing placement. */
    layers: default toast;
    /* The screen itself NEVER scrolls: the transcript is the scrolling
     * surface and the input panel is docked, so a scrollbar here can only
     * come from a floating overlay. It did. A tall `/usage` card (or any
     * overlay taller than its resting offset allows) pushed the screen's
     * virtual height past its size, which drew a scrollbar the app has no
     * use for AND cost two cells of width — the transcript reflowed behind
     * a popup that is supposed to leave the layout alone. Layers stop the
     * overlay taking a row in the FLOW; only this stops it extending the
     * scrollable region. */
    overflow: hidden;
}

/* Transcript takes all the space above the input panel. One cell of edge
 * padding on every side INCLUDING the top: the first line of a turn sitting
 * flush against the terminal's top edge reads as clipped rather than as the
 * start of the conversation. The scrollbar column is reserved permanently
 * (D27) so the right-aligned columns measure stable width. */
TranscriptView {
    height: 1fr;
    /* Right padding is 0 (D21): the permanently reserved scrollbar column
     * below already supplies the right-hand cell, so `1` there made every
     * background-filled tool card one cell wider on the left than the right —
     * visible as an off-centre slab on the one element whose whole job is to
     * look deliberately borderless.
     *
     * The BOTTOM row is the conversation's last row of ground, and it was the
     * one edge of the transcript nobody had spent. Measured at 60x40 with a
     * conversation tall enough to reach the dock: 0 rows between the last
     * transcript row and the composer's fill, so `▌ now do the other one`,
     * `! interrupted` and a failed tool card's tinted slab each sat directly
     * on the panel — the conversation and the input read as one slab.
     *
     * The row belongs to the TRANSCRIPT rather than to `#input-dock`, and that
     * is load-bearing rather than cosmetic:
     *   - the aside already reserves this same padding for its card's rows
     *     (`OperatorApp._reserve_ground_for_aside`), so one owner keeps the
     *     flush card-on-composer seam a single number: the reservation
     *     REPLACES this row instead of stacking with it;
     *   - every floating card is placed off the docked panel's `region.y`
     *     (`widgets/overlay.rows_above_dock`), so a padding row on the DOCK
     *     lifts the aside and `/usage` cards a row off the composer. Measured
     *     at 120x40: it put a blank row into the aside's seam, the one join
     *     that was deliberately chosen flush;
     *   - it is the same semantics as the top row above — the conversation's
     *     own first and last row of ground, scrolling with the content rather
     *     than a permanent inset on the frame. It survives `scroll_end`
     *     because a container's padding is inside its scrollable region.
     * The boot layout takes it back (`Screen.boot TranscriptView`), where the
     * column is bottom-aligned and the row could only ever be slack. */
    padding: 1 0 1 1;
    scrollbar-size-vertical: 1;
    scrollbar-gutter: stable;
    scrollbar-background: $lo-bg;
    /* `edge`, not `dim` (D4). The gutter is reserved permanently so the
     * right-aligned columns measure a stable width, which means the THUMB is
     * on screen whenever the transcript overflows by even one row. At `dim`
     * that is a full-cell saturated column running the height of the view —
     * the largest continuous fill in a narrow frame, reading as the right-hand
     * border a borderless design must not have. `edge` keeps it structurally
     * present; hover still lights it to `dim`. */
    scrollbar-color: $lo-edge;
    /* Rest -> hover -> grabbed is a two-step neutral escalation, no accent.
     * `dim` for hover, not the old `muted`: `muted` is near-text brightness, a
     * large jump that read louder than a *grabbed* bar should — hover brighter
     * than the held state is backwards. `dim` hover -> `muted` grabbed is a
     * clear, monotone rest<hover<grabbed ramp on the 1-cell thumb.
     *
     * The architect proposed `$lo-accent` (the one green) for grabbed; design
     * review (round 1, D1) REJECTED it and settled on neutral `$lo-muted`. Line
     * ~367 reserves the accent for "a turn is live" and nothing else, and a
     * green thumb on a passive scroll gesture would fire that live-activity
     * signal falsely and paint the highest-chroma colour on the reserved gutter
     * (the "saturated column reads as a right border" failure D4 forbids). Keep
     * this ramp neutral. */
    scrollbar-color-hover: $lo-dim;
    scrollbar-color-active: $lo-muted;
}

/* Layer B (the open-hand hover cursor) is NOT declared here. A
 * `TranscriptView > ScrollBar { pointer: grab; }` rule matches the selector but
 * is dead at runtime: system scrollbars are styled through Textual's shared
 * style-cache branch (`Stylesheet.update_nodes`), which never delivers this
 * rule to the widget — the computed `pointer` stays `default` through compose,
 * scroll and resize (verified in review round 1, M1). The pointer is instead
 * set as an INLINE style in `TranscriptView.on_mount`, which bypasses that
 * cache; see the comment there. The drawn colour ramp above is the real,
 * everywhere-visible affordance regardless. */

/* Blocks: zero outer margin, zero padding — no declarations here by design
 * (the minimalism defense asserts their absence; anything uniform here would
 * be the "blank row between everything" regression). */
TranscriptBlock, UserBlock, NoticeBlock, RichBlock, AssistantBlock, ToolCard, WakeBlock {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
}

/* The ONLY separator in the app: one blank row, applied per block by the
 * transcript container's adaptive spacing rule. */
.gap-above {
    margin-top: 1;
}

/* The prompt's own ink. The gutter bar and the prose are both styled
 * per-span by the widget (see `UserBlock` in widgets/transcript.py, which
 * owns the treatment because it has to wrap the text itself to keep the bar
 * on every row); this is the base the sheet would fall back to and the one
 * place a reader grepping the stylesheet for "user" finds the pointer.
 *
 * NO background. A `tint-user` ground shipped briefly and was removed on
 * review: a tinted band behind every prompt reads as a slab in a transcript
 * whose only other fill is the tool ledger, and the two competed. The
 * gutter bar already marks a prompt on every wrapped row, and the adaptive
 * `.gap-above` rule already brackets it, so the ground was a third cue for
 * something two were carrying. What the removal keeps from that argument:
 * the ledger's fill carries the OUTCOME, and nothing else in the transcript
 * should wear a fill that could be mistaken for it. */
UserBlock {
    color: $lo-fg;
}

/* The prompt's half of `display.comfortable_rows`, so a prompt and an action
 * row keep one vertical rhythm rather than the setting splitting them.
 *
 * VERTICAL ONLY, and that is a constraint rather than a preference. The block
 * wraps its own text (the gutter bar has to land on every wrapped row) and
 * measures with `self.size.width`, which INCLUDES horizontal padding, while
 * `render()` is handed the CONTENT width. Left/right padding here would leave
 * the widget wrapping to a width two cells wider than it is given, and the
 * prompt renders as a tinted band with no prose in it. Top/bottom padding is
 * safe: the block is `height: auto` and writes its own height from the row
 * count, so vertical padding grows it instead of clipping it. */
.comfortable-rows UserBlock {
    padding: 1 0 0 0;
}

NoticeBlock {
    color: $lo-dim;
}

RichBlock {
    color: $lo-fg;
}

/* Assistant markdown renders normal weight in the base ink color. */
AssistantBlock {
    color: $lo-fg;
}

/* DRAG-SELECTION band (TUI-021). The one place a background is allowed to mean
 * nothing about the content under it — it marks a RANGE, and the range moves.
 *
 * Textual's default here is `textual-dark`'s `ansi_bright_blue` on
 * `ansi_black` (see `theme.py`, `screen-selection-background`), which is the
 * only saturated blue anywhere in the app and lands on prose the reader is
 * mid-gesture over. `$lo-faint` is the answer for the one reason that
 * distinguishes it: the band has to be visible on BOTH grounds it can fall on,
 * the island `bg` and the tool ledger's `surface` fill, while leaving the body
 * ink readable on top of itself. Measured on the dark ramp — `faint` is 1.97:1
 * against `bg` and 1.81:1 against `surface` with `fg` at 7.58:1 on it (light
 * ramp: 2.08, 1.90, 7.28). `edge` fails the first test (1.55 and 1.42 — a band
 * you cannot find over a tool card) and `dim` fails the second (`fg` at
 * 3.29:1, under the 4.5:1 floor for body text).
 *
 * The ink is NORMALISED to `fg` rather than left alone, which does flatten the
 * markdown tints inside the band — inline code loses its `signal`, a heading
 * its step. That is what a selection is: a mode the reader is holding for a
 * moment, not a re-tint of the document, and it is what every terminal and
 * editor does. Stating it also takes the choice away from Textual, whose
 * default put `ansi_black` here.
 *
 * Written as the desugared descendant form because that is what `Screen`'s own
 * `& > .screen--selection` compiles to; app CSS outranks widget DEFAULT_CSS in
 * Textual's cascade, so this wins without a specificity fight. */
Screen > .screen--selection {
    background: $lo-faint;
    color: $lo-fg;
}

/* ONE LINE PER ACTION — the row is a background-filled card, one elevation
 * step brighter than the ground (kit surface on bg).
 *
 * The collapsed height is PINNED rather than left to `auto`, and that is
 * load-bearing: a card is built before it is laid out, so its very first
 * row is measured against a guessed width and can be a cell or two wide.
 * Under `auto` that first measurement resolves to two rows and stays there
 * even after the resize rebuilds the row to fit — a ledger of one-line
 * traces silently doubles in height. Pinning makes the worst case a clipped
 * cell for one frame, which is the right failure. */
ToolCard, WakeBlock {
    height: 1;
    background: $lo-surface;
    /* The hand pointer, not just the hover tint: the whole row is the click
     * target for expand/collapse (see ToolCard.on_click / WakeBlock.on_click),
     * so the cursor is the affordance that says so BEFORE the click. Textual
     * walks this rule on every mouse event and emits OSC 22, which ghostty
     * implements and a terminal that does not simply ignores. */
    pointer: pointer;
}

/* Expanded is the ONE case that is content-sized: the summary row plus one
 * row per line of revealed output (each already truncated to width, so
 * nothing reflows). The class beats the type selector above. WakeBlock uses
 * its own class name because it is a different widget; the height contract
 * is the same. */
ToolCard.tool-expanded, WakeBlock.wake-expanded {
    height: auto;
}

/* COMFORTABLE DENSITY (`display.comfortable_rows`, default ON changed to OFF by maintainer). One padding
 * row above and below an action's summary.
 *
 * This is a HIT TARGET, not decoration. The whole row is the click surface
 * for expand/collapse (`ExpandableActionBlock.on_click` is on the widget, so
 * padding rows are inside it), and at one cell tall that target was a single
 * terminal line: a click a row off either landed on a neighbouring action or
 * fell through to the transcript. Three rows triples the target without
 * moving a single glyph — the summary is still exactly one row of text, so
 * the ledger's one-line-per-action reading is unchanged.
 *
 * `height: 2` is `1 + padding-top`. Textual sizes a fixed-height widget
 * INCLUDING its padding, so the two move together or the summary clips. The
 * class carries BOTH or neither.
 *
 * TOP ONLY, and that is a correctness requirement rather than a taste call.
 * The dock guarantees EXACTLY ONE ground row between the last block and the
 * composer (`test_composer_seam`, which counts blank rows by walking the
 * composed frame). A bottom padding row is blank, so a padded last block read
 * as a five-row seam and the guarantee broke. Padding the top only buys the
 * same separation between neighbouring rows — every row still gains a cell of
 * air above it — while the block still ENDS on its summary, so the seam below
 * the ledger is the dock's one row and nothing else.
 *
 * The cost is real: a twelve-row ledger occupies thirty-six rows, so less
 * history fits on a screen. That is why this is a setting rather than a
 * decision made for everyone — a user who reads a long ledger more often
 * than they click one turns it off.
 *
 * Expanded rows are exempt: they are already `height: auto` and content-sized,
 * and the payload gives the pointer plenty to hit. */
.comfortable-rows ToolCard, .comfortable-rows WakeBlock {
    height: 2;
    padding: 1 0 0 0;
}

.comfortable-rows ToolCard.tool-expanded, .comfortable-rows WakeBlock.wake-expanded {
    height: auto;
    padding: 1 0 0 0;
}

/* State reaches the GROUND, not just the glyph: a live action sits one step
 * proud of its settled neighbours, and a failure stops being neutral. Both
 * tints sit near the surface luminance so the ledger never shouts. */
ToolCard.tool-running {
    background: $lo-raised;
}

ToolCard.tool-error {
    background: $lo-tint-danger;
}

/* Mouse affordance: the whole row is the click target for expand/collapse,
 * so the whole row lights on hover. Last rule wins over the state tints —
 * pointing at a row should always look the same. */
ToolCard:hover, WakeBlock:hover, .interactive-notice:hover {
    background: $lo-overlay;
}

/* Keyboard affordance, and the reason the card is a focus stop at all: a row
 * the arrows have landed on has to look landed-on, or Enter is a guess.
 *
 * `tint-select` rather than the hover `overlay` on purpose. They are two
 * different pointers — the mouse is where the hand is, focus is where the
 * KEYBOARD is, and only one of them survives the hand leaving the mouse — so
 * a focused row under a wandering pointer must not lose its mark. The tint is
 * the same cool-green ground the command picker gives its highlighted row,
 * which is already this app's word for "this is the one you are on".
 *
 * Last rule in the block, so it wins over the state tints AND over :hover:
 * where the keyboard is, is the more load-bearing fact. Still no border —
 * elevation is a background step here as everywhere. */
ToolCard:focus, WakeBlock:focus, .interactive-notice:focus {
    background: $lo-tint-select;
}

/* The status band is the LAST row of the input panel, below the editor. It reads
 * as a footer for the whole app rather than a caption on the input, and it puts
 * the least volatile information at the screen's least volatile edge.
 *
 * SAME fill as the input panel, not a step down. A second elevation inside one
 * panel read as two stacked bars — a strip of chrome bolted under the input
 * rather than part of it. The panel is one surface; the band is simply its last
 * row, and the blank row above separates them.
 *
 * That blank row is PADDING rather than a vertical margin — the stylesheet
 * permits exactly one such declaration (the adaptive `.gap-above` rule) and a
 * second source of it is how adaptive spacing decays into "a blank row between
 * everything". Because the band and the panel share a fill, a padded row is
 * visually identical to a gap. */
#status-band {
    dock: bottom;
    height: 2;
    background: $lo-surface;
    color: $lo-dim;
    /* Left padding 0: the dock already insets this panel by one cell, so a
     * symmetric `0 1` stacked a second cell on and put the band's leading glyph
     * at column 2 while the input chevron directly above it, the tool glyphs and
     * the prose all start at column 1. The app has one strong left rail and the
     * bottom row was the only thing missing it. */
    padding: 1 1 0 0;
}

/* Input panel: two widgets for one panel, and the split is load-bearing.
 *
 * `#input-dock` is a POSITIONER — docked to the bottom, full width, no fill of
 * its own. It stays docked in both layouts because a docked child's rows are
 * reserved by the layout engine before anything else is placed: the input is
 * the one widget on screen that may never be clipped or pushed off, and in the
 * flow it would be, on any terminal too short for the splash above it.
 *
 * `#input-shell` is the panel the user sees: the fill, the padding, and — in the
 * boot layout — the clamp that turns it into a centred card. It has to be a
 * separate widget because Textual places a docked child at its edge and its
 * parent's `align-horizontal` cannot move it, so a clamped DOCK would sit
 * against the left edge of the terminal rather than centred. The dock centres
 * this child instead, which in the full-width layout is a no-op.
 *
 * No border of any kind. The elevation step from the transcript's `bg` to this
 * panel's `surface` is the whole separation — a `border-top` rule read as a
 * bottom border on the app, which is exactly the bordered-chrome look the kit
 * exists to avoid. If the panel ever stops reading as separate, the answer is
 * another elevation step, never a line.
 *
 * Padding on all four sides: the editor text used to start flush against the
 * panel's edges, which read as an unfinished box rather than a deliberate
 * borderless field. One cell of breathing room is what makes "no border" look
 * chosen instead of missing. */
#input-dock {
    dock: bottom;
    height: auto;
    align-horizontal: center;
}

#input-shell {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
    background: $lo-surface;
    padding: 1;
}

#input-row {
    height: auto;
}

#prompt-chevron {
    width: 2;
    height: auto;
    color: $lo-dim;
}

/* Focus BRIGHTENS the chevron — the rule stays dim (D23), and so does the hue.
 *
 * `fg`, not `$lo-accent`. The accent means "a turn is live" and nothing else,
 * and the composer is focused in essentially every frame a user looks at, so
 * an accent chevron was a permanent green two rows above the band's streaming
 * spinner — three accent sites on one frame carrying two different meanings,
 * and the always-on one the loudest (D5, round 11).
 *
 * Brightness says it better anyway, measured against this panel's `surface`:
 * `fg` 13.76:1 over `dim` 4.18:1 is a 3.86x luminance step and 3.29:1 BETWEEN
 * the two states, clear of the 3:1 non-text floor. Accent over dim was 2.15x
 * and 1.92:1 — accent and `muted` are within 1.3% of each other in luminance
 * (0.4365 vs 0.4309), so the old focus signal was carried almost entirely by
 * HUE and all but vanished for a red-green deficient reader. It also lands on
 * exactly the ink the caret is drawn in, so a focused composer states itself
 * once, in one colour, at both ends of its own row.
 *
 * Driven by a CLASS the app toggles (see `OperatorApp._sync_composer_focus`),
 * NOT by `#input-dock:focus-within`, which silently never turned back off.
 * Textual re-applies styles on a focus change by walking up from the widget
 * that gained or lost focus until it finds an ancestor whose rules mention
 * `focus-within` (`Screen._update_focus_styles`), and that flag is recorded on
 * the node the rule SELECTS — here the chevron, which is the editor's SIBLING
 * and so is never on that walk. Measured on the shipped build: after the boot
 * `editor.focus()`, blurring the composer left `:focus-within` reporting False
 * while the chevron still painted on every later frame. Class changes go
 * through `App.update_styles`, which re-applies the node AND its descendants,
 * so the chevron follows the class in both directions. */
#input-dock.-composer-focused #prompt-chevron {
    color: $lo-fg;
}

/* Bang-mode recolors the marker AND swaps the glyph (`❯` → `$`; see
 * `OperatorApp.on_shell_mode_changed`). Colour is the redundant cue for
 * readers who can see it; the glyph is the one that survives `NO_COLOR`,
 * a monochrome terminal, and red-green colour vision deficiency (issue 385).
 * `string` is the ramp's command/success green — a step off the accent,
 * so a shell-mode composer does not spend the "turn is live" ink (D5).
 * Outranks the focus brightness: the mode is the louder fact, and a
 * focused shell-mode marker that went `fg` would look identical to a
 * focused prompt on a colour-capable terminal. */
#input-dock.-composer-shell #prompt-chevron {
    color: $lo-string;
}

Editor {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
    min-height: 1;
    max-height: 8;
    border: none;
    background: $lo-surface;
    color: $lo-fg;
}

/* TextArea component styling (theme "css" reads these from the widget). */
Editor .text-area--cursor {
    color: $lo-surface;
    background: $lo-fg;
}

/* The composer's selection band.
 *
 * The INK is pinned, not merely the ground. Setting only `background` left the
 * selected run on Textual's own built-in default, a near-white that is a
 * dark-theme assumption masquerading as a neutral: on the light ramp it landed
 * at 1.003:1 against this band, so selected text did not highlight, it ERASED
 * — a triple-click made the whole draft look deleted, which is the one
 * sentence the multi-click gesture exists to stop a user saying (design round
 * 1, D1). Naming `$lo-fg` keeps the selected run in the ramp's own prose ink,
 * so the band changes the GROUND and never the legibility of the text on it.
 *
 * Checked across all 35 registered themes, not just the two brand ramps, since
 * this rule is shared by every one of them: the weakest is `one-dark` at
 * 6.02:1 and the light ramp reads 12.63:1, all clear of AA.
 *
 * The ground stays `edge` deliberately. It is the ramp's documented "selected
 * prose" ground, and the attachment chip's colours are calibrated AGAINST it —
 * the resting chip is iso-luminant with `edge` so it never outranks a real
 * selection, and the selected chip sits a further step above it (see
 * `.text-area--attachment-marker` below). Swapping the band for another
 * tint would silently retune that relationship. */
Editor .text-area--selection {
    color: $lo-fg;
    background: $lo-edge;
}

Editor .text-area--placeholder {
    color: $lo-dim;
}

Editor .text-area--gutter {
    color: $lo-dim;
    background: $lo-surface;
}

Editor .text-area--cursor-line {
    background: $lo-surface;
}

Editor .text-area--suggestion {
    color: $lo-dim;
}

/* An attachment marker — `[Image #1, 1568x200]`, `[Paste #2, 240 lines]` — is
 * an OBJECT the composer put in the buffer, not text the user typed, and it
 * used to render in the prose colour on the prose ground with nothing to say so.
 *
 * ONE style for both payload shapes. The rationale below is about
 * attachment-ness, not image-ness, so a second colour for the paste chip would
 * be a second way of saying one thing; the class is named for what it marks.
 *
 * A chip, not coloured text: syntax-highlighting the token would still read as
 * text, where a ground behind it reads as something embedded. The ink is
 * `signal`, the ramp's file/reference hue, which is also the one colour the
 * composer has not already spent — the chevron one column left is accent
 * green, and `edge` behind selected prose is the warm side.
 *
 * Not a focus ring and not a selection: the chip is iso-luminant with `edge`
 * (1.04:1) so it never outranks a real selection sitting beside it, and the
 * SELECTED chip is a further 1.61:1 up, so clicking one always brightens. */
Editor .text-area--attachment-marker {
    color: $lo-signal;
    background: $lo-tint-attach;
}

Editor .text-area--attachment-marker-selected {
    color: $lo-fg;
    background: $lo-tint-attach-hi;
}

/* ── slash-command syntax highlighting ───────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Foreground-only tints on the composer's leading `/command` word and, for
 * `/team`·`/agent`, the recognized team/agent NAME. The point is legibility of
 * intent: the user sees which tokens are the recognized command and its
 * argument NAME (structure that will NOT be sent as message text) versus the
 * free-text tail that will. Foreground-only so the tint composes cleanly with
 * the cursor's inverse and the selection ground without a competing background
 * — see `Editor._paint_slash`. Component classes reading `$lo-*` (never a
 * Python hex) so a `/theme` switch repaints them for free, exactly like the
 * attachment chip above. `$lo-accent` is deliberately NOT used here: it is
 * reserved for "a turn is live" (see this file's header), and a recognized
 * command word is structure, not activity. */
/* A recognized slash command. `signal` is the ramp's cool file/reference hue
 * (already the chip's ink), which reads as "structured token", not the accent
 * green of a live turn. Bold lifts the short word above the prose around it. */
Editor .text-area--slash-command {
    color: $lo-signal;
    text-style: bold;
}
/* The recognized team/agent NAME after the command word. `string` (the
 * success/string green) distinguishes the resolved argument from the command
 * word so the two reads never collapse into one, without a second ground. */
Editor .text-area--slash-argument {
    color: $lo-string;
}
/* A leading /word that resolves to NO command: dimmed so the user sees it is
 * inert text that WILL be sent, not a recognized command. `dim`, not a danger
 * colour — an unknown slash is a typo-in-progress, not an error to alarm about
 * (and it is suppressed entirely while the command picker is still choosing the
 * word). `dim` over `muted` (design round 1, D1): against the prose tail (`fg`)
 * `muted` was only ~1.74:1 apart — the eye read it as "slightly dimmer", not
 * "inert / will be sent". `dim` lands ~2.9:1 below prose and ~4.18:1 above the
 * input ground, so the degrade-to-text read is unmistakable without tipping
 * into an alarm colour. */
Editor .text-area--slash-unknown {
    color: $lo-dim;
}

/* ── startup toast ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Top-right transient overlay. The host HUGS its card (`width: auto`) and both
 * are positioned by a Python-set offset and `max-width` (see widgets/toast.py —
 * both depend on the terminal width, which a stylesheet cannot ask for).
 *
 * The host must never be wider than the card. Textual hands a widget its whole
 * region and blanks all of it, so `width: 1fr` here painted the card's 35 cells
 * of notice and erased the remaining 59 cells of transcript on the same rows —
 * the layer keeps the toast out of the layout, not off the screen.
 *
 * Top rather than bottom-right, which is where Textual's own notifications go:
 * the input dock and status band are docked to the bottom, so a bottom toast
 * would cover the editor the user is typing into.
 *
 * The card is BORDERLESS and FILLED — elevation is a background step, never a
 * line (D4). It takes `overlay`, the ramp's top step, because it floats above
 * every other surface; `ToolCard` is the same treatment one step lower. The
 * one cell of horizontal padding is the app's edge-padding unit, so the text
 * does not touch the fill's edge.
 *
 * `height: auto` because the message is one or two lines and the widget
 * decides which — unlike the tool card, this content is measured only once per
 * toast and is never re-flowed mid-life, so the auto-height trap that pins the
 * card does not apply here. */
#toast-host {
    layer: toast;
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
}

Toast {
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 1;
    background: $lo-overlay;
    color: $lo-fg;
    /* A click dismisses the card early (Toast.on_click) — the hand pointer
     * says so, the same affordance the tool rows and subagent rows carry. */
    pointer: pointer;
}

/* ---- usage panel (the `/usage` popup) --------------------------------- *
 *
 * Same treatment as the toast, and for the same reasons: it floats above every
 * other surface, so it takes `overlay` (the ramp's top step) as a FILL, with no
 * border — elevation is a background step, never a line (D4).
 *
 * It differs from the toast in what it is FOR, not in how it is placed. It is
 * read rather than glanced at, so it holds focus and is dismissed by a key
 * instead of a timer: nothing about the session changes underneath a table the
 * user is reasoning about.
 *
 * The host HUGS the panel (`width: auto`) and both are positioned by a
 * Python-set offset — the identical rule the toast host carries, for the
 * identical reason. Textual hands a widget its whole region and BLANKS all of
 * it, so a `width: 100%` host on this layer erases every column of every row
 * the panel covers, including the transcript text either side of it. The layer
 * keeps the panel out of the LAYOUT; it does not keep it off the screen. That
 * also rules out `align: center middle` here, which needs a stretched host —
 * the centring is arithmetic in the widget instead, against the measured
 * screen it already has to read to size its own bars. */
#usage-host {
    layer: toast;
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
}

/* `padding: 1 2`, not `0 1`: this card floats OVER the transcript, so its
 * gutter is the only thing separating the report from the text underneath it.
 * One cell was not enough for the panel to read as lifted — the rows touched
 * the fill's edge and the whole card looked cramped. The widget mirrors both
 * numbers in PANEL_PADDING_CELLS/ROWS because it sizes itself (Textual's
 * width/height are border-box), so a change here MUST change those. */
UsagePanel {
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    padding: 1 2;
    background: $lo-overlay;
    color: $lo-fg;
}

/* On a terminal too short for the card's normal two-row vertical gutter, the
 * widget spends the decoration rather than cover the docked prompt. The class
 * is set from the SAME `_fit()` result that pins the height, so CSS and Python
 * cannot disagree about whether those rows exist. Horizontal breathing stays. */
UsagePanel.-squeezed {
    padding: 0 2;
}

/* ---- the /btw aside card --------------------------------------------- *
 *
 * Same layer, same host rule, same reason as `#usage-host` above: `width:
 * auto` so the host hugs the card, because a stretched host on an overlay
 * layer blanks every cell of every row it covers — including the transcript
 * either side of the card. Placement is a Python-set offset on the host
 * (`widgets/overlay.anchor_bottom` — bottom-anchored rather than centred,
 * because this card GROWS while it is being read and a centred one would drag
 * the text with it), which is also why there is no `align` here.
 *
 * It differs from the usage card in ONE way that matters to the stylesheet:
 * this card does not take focus. The composer keeps it, because the user is
 * meant to keep typing into the aside, so nothing here may compete for it. */
#aside-host {
    layer: toast;
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
}

/* One cell of horizontal padding is `#input-shell`'s, not the usage card's
 * `1 2`, and that is the point: the card sits directly ON the composer and
 * shares its column, so the same inset puts the card's `▌` spine in the same
 * cell as the composer's `❯`, at every width and in both layouts. (The card's
 * PROSE then sits one cell left of the editor's typed text, because Textual's
 * `TextArea` carries an inherited `padding: 0 1` the composer never overrides
 * — the card lines up with the TRANSCRIPT's user block instead, which is
 * where `^f` forks the question to.)
 *
 * One padding row on every side. The seam between the hint row and the `❯`
 * measures 2 rows, but the fill change falls BETWEEN them, so the card
 * contributes one and the composer contributes one and neither surface holds
 * a 2-row interval — the unit that has to stay on a 1-row rhythm is each
 * SURFACE, not the cell column across a fill boundary. That is the refinement
 * of an earlier measurement here which was right about the 2 rows and one
 * level too coarse about what they meant.
 *
 * Measured at 120x40 without the bottom row, the hint row was simultaneously
 * the last text row and the card's last row, so the fill terminated on an
 * inked line while every other row had fill above and below it — and the row
 * denied that air was the one telling the user how to leave. With the dock
 * band populated the same hint row abutted the band's heading with zero rows
 * between two different widgets.
 *
 * The widget mirrors both numbers in PANEL_PADDING_CELLS/ROWS because it sizes
 * itself (Textual is border-box), so a change here MUST change those.
 *
 * `$lo-overlay` over the composer's `$lo-surface` — one elevation step apart,
 * which is how this kit separates stacked surfaces. No border and no rule
 * between them: the step IS the seam. Dropping the card to the composer's
 * fill and marking the boundary with a line was tried and measured: it puts
 * the card/ground edge at 1.088:1, the exact figure this sheet already calls
 * invisible where it chose `$lo-overlay` in the first place, and a rule on the
 * card's LAST row is its bottom edge — it closes the shape rather than marking
 * a margin, which is the line this file's own contract draws. The step is also
 * what makes the bottom padding row affordable: without it the two rows fall
 * inside ONE fill and become a real hole. */
AsidePanel {
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    padding: 1;
    background: $lo-overlay;
    color: $lo-fg;
}

/* Too short for the vertical gutter: spend the decoration rather than cover
 * the docked prompt. Set from the same `_fit()` result that pins the height. */
AsidePanel.-squeezed {
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* No `pointer: pointer`, and no click targets. Textual 8.2.8 supports the
 * property, so this says NO deliberately rather than by omission. The card
 * owns no input by contract — the ONE composer is pointed at it — so a hit
 * area would be a second input surface competing for focus the aside
 * deliberately leaves in the editor, and `esc`/`^f` are modal keys with no
 * sensible region to click. Clicks are swallowed (`AsidePanel.on_click`); a
 * pointer cursor over a surface that stops every click is a promise the card
 * does not keep. The one mouse gesture that DOES act is the wheel, which
 * walks back through earlier questions. */

/* The conversation recedes while the aside owns the composer. Same rule as
 * the subagent page below — what recedes is what is INERT, and with Enter
 * aimed at the card the transcript is exactly that. It also settles the
 * fragment noise: an 88-cell card over a 120-cell screen leaves ~16 cells of
 * mid-word transcript down each side, interleaving with the card's own rows
 * at full brightness.
 *
 * `opacity`, NOT the `text-opacity` the subagent rule uses: measured, the
 * latter does not reach the mounted transcript blocks (their ink stayed at
 * the full `fg` token) while `opacity` does. 60% rather than the subagent
 * page's 45%, and the difference is the point — an aside is a question ABOUT
 * the conversation, so it has to stay readable behind the card. 60% puts it
 * at 5.92:1 against the card's 11.24:1 answer text; 45% drops it to 3.84:1,
 * too far for content the user may still be reading. */
Screen.aside #transcript {
    opacity: 60%;
}

/* ---- welcome view (new-session empty state) --------------------------- *
 *
 * Lives INSIDE the transcript, so it is handed exactly the region above the
 * input panel without any arithmetic here.
 *
 * `auto`, not `1fr`: in the boot layout below, the splash RESTS ON the input
 * card, and a stretched block cannot rest on anything — it reports the whole
 * region as its height, so the block floats in the middle of it with the card
 * an arbitrary number of rows further down. Content-sized, the widget reports
 * exactly the rows its block occupies (build_welcome_lines returns no padding
 * rows) and the alignment below puts that block against the card. It also
 * settles the /clear case the old `1fr` was chosen for: the "history is
 * untouched" notice mounted under this view now simply follows the block
 * instead of competing with it for the region.
 *
 * The region it is measured against already excludes the input panel's rows,
 * because the panel is DOCKED and the layout engine reserves a docked child's
 * rows before it offers the rest to the flow. That is what keeps the block's
 * height budget honest without any arithmetic here or in the widget.
 *
 * No border, no rule, no fill: the block is the only thing on screen, so the
 * mandate's "structure from symbols, tint and spacing" is all it gets. The
 * accent green is NOT spent here — the logo lockup carries its own hierarchy
 * through the neutral ramp (mark `dim`, wordmark `fg`).
 *
 * HORIZONTAL centring is still computed in the widget, not declared here: what
 * it draws depends on the cells it actually has (the logo tier drops before the
 * hints, which drop before the status rows), so it needs the measured width
 * that `content-align` would hide from it. VERTICAL placement is the
 * stylesheet's alone (`Screen.boot`), so the two can never disagree. */
#welcome {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
    min-height: 0;
    color: $lo-dim;
}

/* ── boot layout: the input is a centred card until the session has content ──
 *
 * Everything above assumes the input panel is a full-width band. Before the
 * transcript holds a single block it is not: the panel is a clamped card, and
 * the splash rests directly on it so the two read as ONE column rather than a
 * logo adrift over a bar. The status band stays the card's last row in both
 * layouts, so it travels with the input it belongs to instead of stranding
 * itself at the bottom of the screen, and the pickers keep their place between
 * the input row and the band — inside the clamp, because they are inside the
 * card.
 *
 * `Screen.boot` is the whole switch, and one place decides it
 * (OperatorApp._sync_boot_layout_class), which re-derives it from live state:
 * the splash is up AND no full-page mode (`/settings`, the subagent view, the
 * org chart) has replaced the transcript region. Two layouts, ONE condition: a
 * second notion of "the session is empty" is exactly how a centred boot card
 * ends up on screen beside a populated transcript — or, as the v0.43.0 report
 * showed, on top of a mode that thought it had the whole view. A second
 * class, `.boot-card`, rides on top of it — not a third layout, a MEASUREMENT:
 * whether this terminal is wide enough for the card to read as one (see the rule
 * below).
 *
 * The clamp is `max(75, 70% of the content box)`, never wider than the content
 * box. It takes three declarations because tcss has no `max()`: `width` is the
 * proportion, `min-width` the floor, `max-width` the degradation — and the
 * degradation BEATS the floor, so at 40 or 20 cells the card is simply as wide
 * as there is room for rather than overflowing a terminal the minimalism suite
 * measures. NOTHING is added to make it read as a card: the panel's existing
 * surface step IS the card (D4), now bounded on both sides instead of running
 * the width of the terminal.
 *
 * `align-vertical: bottom` on the TRANSCRIPT is what puts the splash on the
 * card: the transcript keeps its `1fr` height, so the region it offers the
 * splash is still the engine's own "everything the docked panel did not take",
 * and the content-sized block inside it is pushed to the bottom of that region —
 * which is the card's top edge. Making the transcript content-sized instead
 * looks equivalent and is not: its measured height then depends on the block it
 * contains while the block's budget depends on the region, and the pair settles
 * one row past the bottom of the screen, where a scrollbar appears and reflows
 * the width of everything.
 *
 * WHERE the pair sits is not this rule's, though — it only says the splash and the
 * card touch. The pair is the composition, and a composition resting on the bottom
 * of a 48-row terminal left the upper two thirds of the screen empty, so the app
 * reserves rows BELOW the card as well (OperatorApp._sync_boot_composition) and the
 * two slacks come out even. Reserving them is conditional on their being spare:
 * every row the composition takes is a row the splash's budget loses, and the
 * splash pays in whole sections — at 28 rows the block wants all 20 it is offered,
 * and buying air there would spend the mark, the one thing that screen exists to
 * show. So a short terminal keeps exactly the old behaviour: the pair on the
 * bottom, all the slack above, where nothing needs it.
 *
 * The panel stays DOCKED here. In the flow it would be the last thing placed
 * and the first thing clipped, and on a terminal too short for the splash above
 * it, the input — the one widget that may never disappear — is what would walk
 * off the bottom edge.
 *
 * No `transition:` on purpose. The swap is instant: a half-drawn interpolation
 * of a docked panel reads as a glitch in a terminal, not as motion. */
Screen.boot TranscriptView {
    align-vertical: bottom;
    /* The one row the boot layout does not need — and the LEFT cell restated,
     * because Textual resolves `padding` as one value per rule: a lone
     * `padding-top` here would win the whole declaration and take the spine's
     * left cell with it, moving every row in the region one column out of line
     * with the input chevron below.
     *
     * In the conversation layout the top row keeps a turn's first line off the
     * terminal's top edge, where it would read as clipped. Here the column is
     * bottom-aligned against the card, so that row can only ever be slack — and
     * the screen's own one-cell inset is still above it, so nothing touches the
     * edge either way. It is not free slack, though: under the shared-region
     * budget (WelcomeView.get_content_height) every row this region spends is a
     * row the splash's degradation ladder has to pay for, and the ladder pays in
     * whole sections. Reclaiming a row that buys nothing is what lets the mark
     * survive a `/clear` receipt AND two failed MCP servers at 96x28. Frames with
     * slack are unchanged: the block is bottom-aligned, so a taller region only
     * grows the empty space above. */
    padding: 0 0 0 1;
}

/* The clamp lives on `.boot-card`, not on `Screen.boot`, because a clamp with no
 * lower guard is worse than no clamp. `max(75, 70%)` insets the panel by 1 to 3
 * cells between 78 and 84 columns — at 80, the commonest terminal, 2 cells on the
 * left and 3 on the right — and an inset smaller than the app's own one-cell
 * gutter does not read as a card. It reads as a full-width bar that is off
 * centre, which is the one thing a borderless surface must never look like:
 * there is no edge to attribute the offset to.
 *
 * So the app measures the card once per resize and adds this class only when the
 * inset is at least 4 cells a side (OperatorApp.on_resize; computing a width in
 * Python is the same move Toast._refit already makes, because a stylesheet cannot
 * ask how wide the terminal is). Below that threshold the panel stays the
 * full-width docked bar the base rule already gives it — the same honest
 * degradation 40 and 20 cells get, arrived at 40 columns earlier.
 *
 * `max-width` is the CAP, in cells, not the old `100%`: unbounded, `70%` resolves
 * to 131 cells on a 190-column terminal and the panel is a bar again — a
 * borderless fill reads as a card only while the ground beside it is legible as
 * margin, and past a hundred cells the fill IS the frame. The `100%` guard it
 * replaces is not lost: the class is only applied where the resolved width leaves
 * at least 8 cells of box, and the geometry suite measures every rendered row
 * against the terminal at 16 and 20 cells, where the floor would overflow.
 *
 * A LIVE PROMPT withholds the class too, and for a reason about COMPOSITION
 * rather than width. This selector clamps `#input-shell` alone; the prompt
 * docked directly above it is `width: 1fr`, and the note on `AskPickerScreen`
 * says why that must stay the ONLY statement of how wide the question is. So
 * the two cannot be clamped together without reintroducing the second width
 * owner that rule was written to remove — and unclamped beside a clamped
 * composer, `Ctrl+L` under a live `ask` hung the question 58 cells past the
 * input it is docked to at 160 columns. The card composition is the empty-state
 * splash resting on the composer, and a question the turn is parked on is about
 * work that already happened, so it is not part of that composition.
 * `OperatorApp._sync_boot_card` is still the one place that decides, so this
 * stays a single condition rather than a counter-rule here.
 *
 * (Issue numbers are spelled out in prose in this file rather than as `#NNN`:
 * the minimalism suite reads that shape as a literal hex colour and rejects the
 * sheet. The Python side carries the numeric references.) */
Screen.boot.boot-card #input-shell {
    width: 70%;
    min-width: 75;
    max-width: 100;
}

/* A system notice under the splash (an MCP server that failed to connect) is
 * part of the centred boot composition, not the full-width spine: left at `1fr`
 * it drew flush against the terminal's left edge while the splash and the card
 * sat centred — one line visibly out of column on the first frame every user
 * with a broken server sees. There is NO rule here on purpose. The block's
 * width and its offset onto the card's column are computed by the app
 * (OperatorApp._sync_boot_column_width) because both are cell counts the sheet
 * cannot ask for, and because `align-horizontal` aligns a container's CHILDREN,
 * never the node carrying it — a rule here would centre nothing. The notice's
 * TEXT is centred by the block itself (NoticeBlock._build), not by `text-align`:
 * the block pins its own height and a stylesheet re-wrap would reflow the
 * hanging indent it cannot see. The `.boot-column` class is only the marker the
 * block reads to decide between the spine and the centred axis. */

/* ── slash-command picker ────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The suggestion list under the editor, inside the input dock and above the
 * bottom-docked status band. Borderless like everything else (D4): the list
 * is held together by the 2-cell selection gutter — which lines up with
 * `#prompt-chevron`, so a highlighted `❯` sits directly beneath the prompt's
 * own `❯` and command names start in the editor's text column. Hence NO left
 * padding here; padding would break that alignment, and a vertical margin is
 * not an option the sheet has (the adaptive `.gap-above` rule is the only
 * vertical margin in the file, deliberately).
 *
 * `height` is written by the widget on every repaint (one row per visible
 * suggestion, plus the overflow count when the list is longer). It is NOT
 * `auto`: a list measured before layout knows its width resolves one row too
 * tall per item and stays there — the same trap `ToolCard` pins itself
 * against. Row backgrounds (selection and hover elevation) are painted into
 * the rich text, since one Static draws every row.
 *
 * The accent lives on the selected command NAME only (D23); this rule paints
 * the ground the unselected rows sit on, which is the dock's own surface so
 * the list reads as part of the input rather than as a popup over it. */
CommandPicker {
    width: 1fr;
    background: $lo-surface;
    color: $lo-fg;
}

/* The tool-approval prompt. Same borderless, filled-card language as the tool
 * ledger, because it IS a row in that ledger's story — the difference is that
 * this one is waiting on the user.
 *
 * Height is content-sized rather than pinned (the exception the ToolCard note
 * above explains): a pending prompt is deliberately two rows, the question and
 * the key hints, and it collapses to one when answered. Both counts come from
 * the widget's own state, not from a width-dependent measurement, so the
 * doubling trap that forced ToolCard's pin cannot happen here.
 *
 * Pending sits one elevation step proud — the same "this is live" signal a
 * running tool row uses — and the amber lives in the TEXT (glyph and keys), so
 * the ground never shouts a colour the palette reserves for warnings. */
ApprovalBlock {
    height: auto;
    background: $lo-surface;
}

ApprovalBlock.approval-pending {
    background: $lo-raised;
}

/* Focused is the state that matters here: the prompt takes focus so its answer
 * keys reach it instead of the composer, and the user must be able to SEE where
 * those keys are going.
 *
 * `tint-select` rather than an elevation step, matching `ToolCard:focus`: it is
 * the app's existing word for "this is the one you are on", and it stays
 * distinguishable from hover (`$lo-overlay`) when both are live on the same row.
 * Two focusable transcript blocks must not spell focus two different ways. */
ApprovalBlock:focus {
    background: $lo-tint-select;
}

/* The login key prompt. Deliberately the SAME language as the approval card
 * above rather than a variation on it: both are "the app is parked on you"
 * blocks that take focus and resolve a future, and giving them two visual
 * idioms would make the user learn the pattern twice.
 *
 * Height is content-sized for the same reason: pending is three or four rows
 * (question, optional instruction, masked value, key hints) and the receipt
 * collapses to one. Every count comes from the widget's own state rather than
 * from a width-dependent measurement. */
KeyPromptBlock {
    height: auto;
    background: $lo-surface;
}

KeyPromptBlock.key-prompt-pending {
    background: $lo-raised;
}

/* Focus matters more here than anywhere else in the transcript: every printable
 * key is captured by this block rather than reaching the composer, so the user
 * has to be able to see where their typing is going before they paste a secret
 * into it. Same `tint-select` the other two focusable transcript blocks use. */
KeyPromptBlock:focus {
    background: $lo-tint-select;
}

/* ── dock band (subagent task list + todo list) ────────────────────────── *
 *
 * Sits between the transcript and the composer: a POSITIONER that reserves
 * rows only when a panel inside it is visible, so an idle session costs the
 * layout nothing, and that carries the dock's fill so the rows it reserves
 * belong to the input rather than to the conversation. The two panels each
 * manage their own `display` (hidden when their ledger/store is empty), and
 * each holds ONE filled body (`$lo-surface`, the tool card's elevation) — the
 * same "elevation is a background step, never a line" rule as everything
 * else. Each slot owns one blank row BELOW itself (see `.band-slot`), and it
 * is padding on the slot, never a margin, so the element that owns the
 * vertical rhythm is the container. */
/* The band is a normal-flow child INSIDE the docked input-dock (D-15-01).
 * `width: auto` (content-sized) so it does NOT consume the dock's full width
 * and defeat the shell's centering below it — a full-width sibling was the
 * reason the boot card sat flush-left. The band's own slots stretch to the
 * input's width via `.band-slot { width: 1fr }`, which resolves against the
 * band's parent width when the band is content-sized. Zero rows when both
 * panels are hidden; the height of the todo/subagent rows otherwise (D-01). */
/* THE FILL RULE, stated once so neither half has to be re-derived: GROUND
 * SEPARATES THE CONVERSATION FROM THE DOCK, NOT THE DOCK'S PANELS FROM THE
 * COMPOSER. The transcript's trailing row is ground and stays ground
 * (`TranscriptView`'s bottom padding, pinned by
 * tests/unit/tui/test_composer_seam.py). Everything from the band's first row
 * down is the dock's own fill, including the rhythm row each `.band-slot`
 * owns below itself — a docked panel is CHROME, and chrome with the
 * transcript showing through it reads as having come loose from the input it
 * belongs to.
 *
 * Measured at 120x40, five todos docked, before this rule: `#todo-body`
 * painted `$lo-surface` on rows 27-32 over columns 1-119, row 33 came back
 * `$lo-bg` over the FULL 0-120 — wider than either slab it sat between,
 * because the slot's padding row is transparent the whole way down to the
 * Screen — and `#input-shell` resumed `$lo-surface` on row 34. One dark row
 * between two lighter ones is a trench, not a margin, and the same single row
 * measured at 60 columns. The subagent slot was further gone: only its header
 * carries `.band-body`, so its ROWS were `$lo-bg` too and the panel read as a
 * lone surface strip floating over a dark list. One fill on the band settles
 * both, and every slot the band is ever given.
 *
 * This lived at `Screen.aside #band` (round 11's D1: the aside card read as
 * floating five rows up, because that same trench sat under it). It was
 * scoped to the aside because `.band-slot` then padded ABOVE itself, so an
 * unconditional fill would have pushed a surface row up against the
 * transcript. The padding flip below moved that row BELOW each slot, so the
 * band's first row is now a panel's own slab and the reason for the scope is
 * gone. An empty band is zero rows and paints nothing, so the splash and
 * every idle session are untouched. */
#band {
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    background: $lo-surface;
}

/* One surface row ABOVE the band's first slot, so a docked panel is inset the
 * same on both sides of its slab.
 *
 * The asymmetry this fixes, measured at 100x24 with three todos: the panel's
 * caption sat directly on the transcript's ground row while TWO surface rows
 * (the last slot's own padding, then `#input-shell`'s) separated its last row
 * from the composer. One row above against two below, on a slab whose whole job
 * is to read as part of the dock — reported from the field as "it looks a bit
 * tight" above the todo and subagent lists.
 *
 * On the BAND, not on `.band-slot`: the slot's padding repeats per slot, and
 * with both panels docked a per-slot top row would open a second gap between
 * the subagent list and the todo list — where the existing single row is
 * already the right rhythm. This is the join between the CONVERSATION and the
 * dock, which happens once no matter how many slots the band holds.
 *
 * Why this does not re-create the trench the padding flip removed: that row was
 * transparent, so it painted the Screen's `$lo-bg` between two lighter slabs.
 * This one is inside `#band` and therefore `$lo-surface` — the same fill as the
 * rows below the slab, so the panel reads as inset within the dock rather than
 * as having come loose from it. The transcript's own ground row still sits
 * above it and still separates the conversation from the dock, which is the
 * rule that row belongs to.
 *
 * Carried by a CLASS the band toggles rather than declared unconditionally,
 * because padding on a container applies even when every child is hidden: an
 * idle session and the boot splash have both panels `display: none`, and an
 * unconditional row would put a bare surface strip into a composition that is
 * measured to the row (`test_an_empty_band_paints_nothing_on_the_splash`). The
 * class is applied only while a slot is actually visible — see
 * `OperatorApp._refresh_band`. */
#band.has-slot {
    padding: 1 0 0 0;
}

/* One blank row UNDER each slot, not over it, and the direction is the whole
 * point: the transcript ends in a row of its own ground (`TranscriptView`'s
 * bottom padding), so a top padding row here stacked with it — measured at
 * 120x40 with a todo list up, 2 blank rows between the conversation's last row
 * and the band's slab against 1 everywhere else in the stack, which is the
 * widest interval on screen and reads as the band having come loose. Downward,
 * each join between the column's own STACKED children is one row: the
 * transcript's trailing row above the band, and this row between two visible
 * panels.
 *
 * The join above the COMPOSER is the exception, and it has two owners: the last
 * visible slot's row here, plus `#input-shell`'s own `padding: 1`. Measured at
 * 60/80/100/120/160 it is two rows docked against one undocked (design round
 * 13, D1-13). Both are surface, so it reads as a slightly deeper gutter rather
 * than a seam, which is why it survived twelve rounds unnoticed — but it is one
 * row more than this comment used to claim. Not fixed here because the obvious
 * spellings do not work: hidden panels keep their place in the DOM
 * (`self.display = False`), so `:last-of-type` targets the wrong slot whenever
 * the subagent panel is down, and dropping the shell's top padding moves the
 * defect to the undocked case. The honest fix belongs in the container that
 * owns this rhythm — `#band` toggling a class onto its last DISPLAYED slot —
 * and is deliberately left out of the release that fixed the fill.
 *
 * What that last row IS changed with the fill rule on `#band`; the geometry did
 * not. It is the dock's own `$lo-surface` now, so band and composer read as one
 * panel with a todo list inside it — which is what was asked for, and what an
 * earlier round argued against while the row was still `$lo-bg`. That argument
 * was right about the slabs and wrong about the remedy: two same-fill slabs
 * touching with NO row between them read as one crowded panel, so the row
 * stays. It is interior rhythm now, the same job the padding row inside the
 * aside card does, and not a boundary. Deleting it crowds the dock; painting it
 * `$lo-bg` again cuts the dock in half.
 *
 * A FLOATING card is the deliberate exception, and the flip made it consistent
 * rather than breaking it. The aside rests on whatever the dock puts at its top
 * (`overlay.stack_on_dock`, gap 0), so with a band up the card's footer now
 * touches the band's slab — measured at 120x40, card footer at row 29 and
 * `Todos · 1/2` at 30. That is the same flush seam the card has on the composer
 * when no band is up, and it is legible for the same reason: the card is
 * `$lo-overlay` over the band's `$lo-surface`, one elevation step, which is
 * this kit's separator. Before the flip the card had a stray ground row under
 * it in this state only, so the one card looked seated on the composer and
 * loose above the band. */
.band-slot {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
    padding: 0 0 1 0;
}

/* The BODY every docked panel is made of, and the reason there is a class
 * rather than two id rules: the fill and the one-cell inset are what make a
 * panel a panel, and a slot that renders its content anywhere else gets
 * neither. `padding: 0 1` is the dock's rail — the same column as the
 * composer's `❯`, the status band's leading glyph and the todo list's `- [ ]`.
 * The subagent panel put this class on its header only, so its ROWS sat on
 * bare ground one cell to the left of everything else in the column: a filled
 * caption over a floating list, four rows above a todo panel that was one
 * solid slab (design round 12, D1/D5). Anything mounted inside a `.band-slot`
 * that is meant to be read as part of the panel wears this. */
.band-body {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
    background: $lo-surface;
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* Clickable in every density (issue 525): the caption is the one widget the panel
 * paints in full AND summary, so it is the pointer's way to cycle the dock
 * where the affordance row (overflow only) does not exist. Same hover ground
 * as `#subagent-affordance` so the two controls read as one kind of thing. */
#subagent-header {
    height: auto;
    color: $lo-dim;
    pointer: pointer;
}

#subagent-header:hover {
    background: $lo-overlay;
}

/* The rows container must SIZE TO ITS CONTENT, never stretch to fill the
 * band. A Vertical defaults to ``1fr``, which absorbs every free row: with
 * even one subagent the band ballooned to the whole screen and crushed the
 * transcript to a single row (D-01). ``height: auto`` ("as tall as my
 * children") keeps the band as tall as its rows — typically one — so the
 * transcript keeps the space above it. Rows themselves are already ``1``
 * tall; the container is what was overeager. (It also carries `.band-body`,
 * which is where its fill and its inset come from; this rule only overrides
 * the height, which `.band-body` already declares the same way.) */
#subagent-rows {
    height: auto;
    overflow-x: hidden;
    overflow-y: auto;
}

/* Pinned below the scrollable roster so the way back never disappears. It is
 * a separate widget for the same reason as the todo control: only the actual
 * affordance gets pointer/hover treatment, never a child row beside it. */
#subagent-affordance {
    height: 1;
    pointer: pointer;
}

#subagent-affordance:hover {
    background: $lo-overlay;
}

/* A row is a live surface: hover lights it, and a running row's trailing cell
 * carries the spinner glyph. Rows are Static; the whole row is the click/Enter
 * target for the full-page subagent view, so hover and focus must read as
 * reachable. */
SubagentRow {
    width: 1fr;
    height: 1;
    /* The whole row opens the page, so the pointer says so before the click
     * does — the same affordance the page's own hints carry. Textual emits
     * this as OSC 22; a terminal that ignores it loses nothing. */
    pointer: pointer;
}

SubagentRow:hover {
    background: $lo-overlay;
}

/* The todo list body is a scroll region, not a bare Static, so an EXPANDED
 * list longer than the panel's height budget stays reachable by scrolling
 * instead of being clipped away under `Screen { overflow: hidden }` (defect 1's
 * overflow case). `height: auto` sizes it to its content in the common case —
 * so a list that fits shows no scrollbar and the dock is exactly as tall as the
 * list — and the `max_height` the panel sets per sync is what caps it to the
 * budget and turns on the scrollbar only when the content genuinely exceeds it.
 * `overflow-y: auto` is the scrollbar-on-demand that pairs with that cap;
 * `overflow-x: hidden` because rows are already truncated to width in
 * `_build` (`_row_cells`) and a horizontal scrollbar would be a second, silent
 * two-cell width loss. It carries `.band-body` for the dock fill and rail. */
#todo-scroll {
    height: auto;
    overflow-x: hidden;
    overflow-y: auto;
}

#todo-body {
    height: auto;
    width: 1fr;
}

/* The collapse/expand affordance is its OWN clickable widget (defect 2), so a
 * `:hover` and an `on_click` can target JUST this row — a click in the list
 * never toggles, and only this row lights on hover. `pointer: pointer` says the
 * row is clickable before the click does (OSC 22, the same affordance
 * `SubagentRow` and the subagent-view hints carry), and the hover ground is
 * `$lo-overlay`, the SAME step `SubagentRow:hover` and `ToolCard:hover` use —
 * "pointing at a row should always look the same". */
#todo-affordance {
    height: 1;
    pointer: pointer;
}

#todo-affordance:hover {
    background: $lo-overlay;
}

/* The row the full-page view is currently showing. The panel stays at full
 * ink in that mode because it is the one thing in the dock that still does
 * something — a row retargets the page — and an interactive list of identical
 * bullets cannot say which one you are reading. `tint-select` is the app's
 * existing word for "this row is the one" (a focused approval carries the
 * same step) and stays distinguishable from the hover tint above. */
Screen.subagent SubagentRow.current {
    background: $lo-tint-select;
}

/* The detail dock now contains only this owner's bounded child preview and
 * plan, not the root's whole roster. Hiding it at 24 rows made nested children
 * unclickable and hid the selected plan. Keep those controls, reclaiming only
 * the decorative inset; each panel still owns its compact/expanded row budget. */
Screen.subagent-compact #band.has-slot {
    padding-top: 0;
}

/* This mode cannot accept a draft: its footer already explains read-only/Esc.
 * Keep the owner's status row but reclaim the disabled input and vertical
 * padding, so exposing its own controls does not reduce the transcript to a
 * single row. Removing the mode restores the parent's untouched draft. */
Screen.subagent-compact #input-row {
    display: none;
}

Screen.subagent-compact #input-shell {
    /* The docked status row does not contribute to an auto-height shell. */
    min-height: 1;
    padding: 0 1;
}

Screen.subagent-compact #status-band {
    height: 1;
    padding: 0;
}

/* ── full-page subagent view ───────────────────────────────────────────── *
 *
 * Opened from a band row (click or Enter). NOT an overlay: it takes the
 * transcript's region of the live screen and leaves the dock exactly where it
 * was, because the reader has to be able to see this is the same app looking
 * somewhere else. Its predecessor was a centred card on the sunken ground,
 * which blacked out the conversation, clipped the child's prose at the card's
 * edge, and had nowhere to put the way back out.
 *
 * Four rows: title, rule, the child's transcript (`1fr`), the hint. The
 * gutter lives HERE rather than on the body, so all four share one left edge;
 * the body's own TranscriptView padding is dropped for the same reason. The
 * right cell is spent so the chrome stops where the body's reserved scrollbar
 * column starts.
 *
 * The BOTTOM cell is this page's share of the transcript's trailing row: the
 * page takes the transcript's region, so it inherits the region's contract that
 * whatever is above the composer ends in a row of ground. Measured at 120x40
 * without it, `↑↓scroll · esc back to conversation · read-only` sat directly on
 * `❯ Read-only — press esc to reply` and the two chrome lines fused into one
 * four-row panel. It cannot come from the body's `TranscriptView` padding,
 * which the class below deliberately zeroes — the hint row is BELOW the body,
 * so the row has to be the page's. */
/* An OPAQUE ground on the scrolling page. Without a fill the container is
 * transparent, and a transparent scrolling region lets Textual's compositor
 * skip clearing cells on a partial repaint: a hover on an `.actionable` hint
 * plus the 8Hz working spinner then painted new cells over stale ones and the
 * page appeared to tear on mouse-move. The `$lo-bg` token matches the Screen's
 * own baseline ground on purpose — subagent mode is "the same app looking
 * elsewhere" (see `_open_subagent_view`), so the fill must read as the app's own
 * ground and not as an elevated slab the way the surface elevation would. */
.subagent-view {
    height: 1fr;
    padding: 1 1 1 1;
    background: $lo-bg;
}

.subagent-view-title {
    height: 1;
}

.subagent-view-breadcrumb {
    height: 1;
}

.subagent-view-rule {
    height: 1;
}

/* The truncation note is page chrome: it sits between the rule and the
 * scrolling body so it cannot leave the screen. Height auto because the
 * wording wraps at narrow widths; display is toggled from the view when
 * the fold first reports the cap. */
.subagent-view-truncation {
    height: auto;
    padding: 1 0 0 0;
}

/* One row of air under the rule. Without it the rule sits directly on the
 * first block, and when that block is a tool card — i.e. whenever the reader
 * has scrolled — a faint full-width line resting on a tinted slab reads as
 * that slab's TOP BORDER, which is the one thing the minimalism contract
 * forbids. The left gutter stays zero: it belongs to the container, so all
 * four rows share one edge. */
.subagent-view-body {
    height: 1fr;
    padding: 1 0 0 0;
}

/* One quiet row above the hint. Without it the footer sits flush against the
 * last transcript block and reads as one more row of the child's output
 * rather than as the page's own chrome — the same reason the usage card keeps
 * ground above its meta. */
.subagent-view-hints {
    height: 2;
    width: 1fr;
    padding: 1 0 0 0;
}

/* Each hint is its own widget so it can be hovered and clicked. Content-sized,
 * because the row is laid out by shedding whole hints rather than by
 * distributing space. The background step is the only part of the hover the
 * stylesheet can own: the hint's ink is chosen per span in rich `Text`, so a
 * `color` rule here would lose to the style the widget builds — the widget
 * repaints its own tones on Enter/Leave, the way a tool card already does.
 * `.actionable` gates it: `read-only` is a state, and a highlight that leads
 * nowhere is worse than none. */
.subagent-view-hint {
    height: 1;
    width: auto;
}

/* The hand pointer, which Textual 8.2.8 DOES support: `styles.pointer` is a
 * real property (`textual.css.constants.VALID_POINTER`), `Screen` walks it on
 * every mouse event, and `App._set_pointer_shape` emits it as OSC 22 — which
 * ghostty implements. The old scope note in docs/VERIFICATION.md searched for
 * `set_mouse_cursor` and a `cursor:` property, neither of which is the name,
 * and concluded the API did not exist. */
.subagent-view-hint.actionable {
    pointer: pointer;
}

/* `$lo-overlay`, the SAME hover ground as `ToolCard:hover` and
 * `SubagentRow:hover` — "pointing at a row should always look the same". On
 * `surface` the step was 1.09:1 against the page ground, roughly a quarter of
 * the app's established hover delta, so the slab was invisible and the whole
 * observable hover was the ink lift. */
.subagent-view-hint.actionable:hover {
    background: $lo-overlay;
}

/* The dock in subagent mode: PRESENT and RECEDED, never removed.
 *
 * `text-opacity` and not `color`, because every one of these paints rich
 * `Text` with its ink already chosen — a `color` declaration would be
 * overridden by the styles the widget builds, and the greying would silently
 * do nothing. Opacity blends whatever was painted toward the ground, which is
 * exactly "this belongs to the session you are not currently reading".
 *
 * 45%: enough that the composer reads as unavailable at a glance, not so much
 * that the status band stops being legible — it is still the parent's live
 * state, and a subagent page is precisely where someone watches for the
 * parent finishing.
 *
 * `#subagent-panel` is deliberately NOT in this list. It is the one thing in
 * the dock that still does something in this mode (a row retargets the page),
 * and the mode's whole visual argument is that what recedes is what is inert. */
Screen.subagent #prompt-chevron,
Screen.subagent #todo-panel {
    text-opacity: 45%;
}

/* The status band is NOT greyed, and the composer is not greyed either.
 *
 * The band carries the parent's live state, including the streaming spinner —
 * accent site 3 of the five. At 45% that green blends to roughly 2.6:1 and the
 * shimmer loses half its amplitude, so the mode would put out the one mark
 * answering the question a reader on this page is most likely waiting on
 * ("has the parent finished?"). The rule the mode paints by is that what
 * recedes is what is INERT; the band is not inert, it is just not yours.
 *
 * The composer keeps its ink because in this mode its placeholder is a
 * SENTENCE about the mode (`Read-only — press esc to reply`), not an
 * invitation, and a statement nobody can read is not a statement. Its
 * chevron carries the greying instead: a dead spine marker over a live one is
 * the whole signal, at no cost to legibility. */

/* The chevron stays DIM in this mode, stated rather than inferred from where
 * focus happens to be. A composer that refuses every key is not one you are
 * typing into, whatever the focus chain says — that is a property of the MODE,
 * so the mode says it. Higher specificity by one TYPE than the focus rule
 * above, so it wins outright whatever the dock's class says. (It also used to
 * be the only thing holding: the focus treatment was driven by
 * `#input-dock:focus-within`, which never turned back off — see that rule for
 * the mechanism.) */
Screen.subagent #input-dock #prompt-chevron {
    color: $lo-dim;
}


/* ── full-page org-chart view ──────────────────────────────────────────── *
 *
 * A sibling MODE of the subagent view (see that block): opened by
 * `/team chart`, it takes the transcript's region and leaves the dock greyed,
 * so the page reads as the same app looking somewhere else. Four rows: title,
 * rule, the chart canvas (`1fr`), the hint.
 *
 * The one deliberate difference from the subagent body is `overflow: auto` on
 * BOTH axes. An org chart is WIDE — wider than the viewport for any real org —
 * so horizontal scroll is legitimate here, unlike the main screen (where a
 * horizontal scrollbar is always a bug) and unlike the subagent body (vertical
 * only). The canvas Static is pinned to the painted grid size, so the
 * container's virtual size equals the canvas and Textual scrolls the
 * difference; the chart is never re-laid-out on scroll. */
.org-chart-view {
    height: 1fr;
    padding: 1 1 1 1;
}

.org-chart-view-title {
    height: 1;
}

.org-chart-view-rule {
    height: 1;
}

/* The `?`-toggled glyph legend (U5/U6). display:none until opened, so it
 * reserves no rows in the resting layout; when shown it takes TWO rows between
 * the rule and the body — glyph vocabulary on the first, the `(declared)` gloss
 * on the second (D7: one line clipped the gloss at standard width). `height:
 * auto` sizes to the content's row count so both rows are always visible. */
.org-chart-view-legend {
    height: auto;
    display: none;
}

/* Both-axes scroll around the canvas. One row of air under the rule, the same
 * reason the subagent body carries it: without it the rule sits on the first
 * box row and a faint full-width line resting on a box reads as chrome that is
 * not there. */
.org-chart-view-body {
    height: 1fr;
    padding: 1 0 0 0;
    overflow: auto;
}

/* The canvas is sized in Python to the painted grid (width AND height), which
 * is what makes the body's virtual size equal the canvas and the scrollbars
 * appear exactly when the org overflows. `auto` here would collapse it to the
 * content the Static was handed with no room to scroll. */
.org-chart-view-canvas {
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
}

/* One quiet row above the hint, same as the subagent footer: without it the
 * hints sit flush on the last canvas row and read as part of the chart. */
.org-chart-view-hints {
    height: 2;
    width: 1fr;
    padding: 1 0 0 0;
}

/* The dock in org-chart mode: PRESENT and RECEDED, never removed — the exact
 * treatment `Screen.subagent` gives it, and for the same reason (what recedes
 * is what is inert; the band stays live because it still carries the parent's
 * state). `text-opacity`, not `color`, because these paint rich `Text` whose
 * ink is already chosen. */
Screen.org-chart #prompt-chevron,
Screen.org-chart #todo-panel {
    text-opacity: 45%;
}

Screen.org-chart #input-dock #prompt-chevron {
    color: $lo-dim;
}


/* ── full-page settings view ───────────────────────────────────────────── *
 *
 * The third sibling MODE (see the org-chart block above): opened by
 * `/settings`, it takes the transcript's region and leaves the dock greyed.
 * Four rows: title, rule, the two-column body (`1fr`), the hints.
 *
 * A MODE and not a ModalScreen specifically because this surface WRITES. The
 * analytics panel may be modal precisely because it "never mutates anything";
 * here the dock is where the status band shows a change landing (a theme
 * switch, a new default model), so blacking it out would hide the effect of
 * the write the user just made. */
.settings-view {
    height: 1fr;
    padding: 1 1 1 1;
}

.settings-view-title {
    height: 1;
}

.settings-view-rule {
    height: 1;
}

/* The two columns. `1fr`/`auto` rather than a percentage split: the settings
 * list is the subject and should take the slack, while the read-only pane is
 * sized by its own content so it never reserves width for rows it does not
 * have (an empty registry would otherwise leave a wide blank gutter). */
.settings-view-columns {
    height: 1fr;
    /* Default quiet row above the list. `SettingsView._apply_height_ladder`
     * CLEARS this padding on a short terminal so the body keeps a setting
     * row — a 1-row shed, so restoring it as the terminal grows never
     * drops the body (issue 431). Do not raise this to 2: a 2-row unit is the
     * non-monotonic move that issue forbids. */
    padding: 1 0 0 0;
}

/* VERTICAL scroll only. Unlike the org chart, whose canvas is legitimately
 * wider than any viewport, this list truncates its rows to the available
 * width — so a horizontal scrollbar here would be the same bug it is on the
 * main screen, two cells of width lost to an axis nothing travels. */
.settings-view-body {
    width: 1fr;
    height: 1fr;
    overflow-x: hidden;
    overflow-y: auto;
    /* The SAME block `TranscriptView` declares, at the same 1 cell against the
     * same `$lo-*` ramp. Declaring `overflow-y: auto` and no `scrollbar-*`
     * rules fell through to Textual's stock styling — a 2-cell saturated blue
     * thumb on a pure-black track, ink that appeared in none of the 35
     * non-settings evidence frames and the highest-chroma thing on a page whose
     * palette is a warm neutral ramp (design round 1, D1). At 80 columns it was
     * exactly the failure the transcript's own note describes: a saturated
     * column running the height of the view, reading as the right-hand border a
     * borderless design must not have.
     *
     * `edge` idle, and NOT `$lo-accent` on active: the transcript's D1 already
     * litigated that and settled on a neutral rest -> hover -> grabbed ramp,
     * because the accent means "THIS is what the turn is on" and a passive
     * scroll gesture is not that. `scrollbar-gutter: stable` reserves the
     * column permanently, which is what makes `_list_width`'s unconditional
     * 2-cell reservation honest rather than a guess about when the bar appears
     * (1 cell of bar plus the gutter's own cell). */
    scrollbar-size-vertical: 1;
    scrollbar-gutter: stable;
    scrollbar-background: $lo-bg;
    scrollbar-color: $lo-edge;
    scrollbar-color-hover: $lo-dim;
    scrollbar-color-active: $lo-muted;
}

/* Pinned in Python to the row count, which is what makes the container's
 * virtual size equal the list and the scrollbar appear exactly when the
 * settings overflow. `auto` would collapse it to the handed content with no
 * room to scroll. */
.settings-view-list {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
}

/* The read-only pane. A FIXED width, not `auto`: the pane truncates its own
 * rows against its measured width, so `auto` was circular — the content
 * shrank to fit the pane and the pane then shrank to fit the content, settling
 * at 19 cells of clipped text. On a narrow terminal the pane is hidden
 * outright by `SettingsView._paint_pane` rather than squeezed, because the
 * settings list is the subject of the page and a 12-cell roster is not
 * readable anyway. */
.settings-view-pane {
    width: 34;
    height: 1fr;
    padding: 0 0 0 3;
}

/* The explanation of the highlighted row. ALWAYS one row, even when empty:
 * a detail line that appeared and disappeared would move the footer, and the
 * body above it, on every cursor move — the "rows are load-bearing" rule. */
.settings-view-detail {
    height: 2;
    padding: 1 0 0 0;
}

/* One quiet row above the hints, same as the org-chart and subagent footers:
 * without it they sit flush on the last setting row and read as one more
 * entry in the list rather than as the page's own chrome. */
.settings-view-hints {
    height: 2;
    width: 1fr;
    padding: 1 0 0 0;
}

/* The dock in settings mode: PRESENT and RECEDED, never removed — the exact
 * treatment `Screen.org-chart` and `Screen.subagent` give it, and for the same
 * reason (what recedes is what is inert; the band stays live because it still
 * carries the session's state, and in THIS mode that is load-bearing — the
 * band is where a theme or model change shows up). `text-opacity`, not
 * `color`, because these paint rich `Text` whose ink is already chosen. */
Screen.settings #prompt-chevron,
Screen.settings #todo-panel {
    text-opacity: 45%;
}

Screen.settings #input-dock #prompt-chevron {
    color: $lo-dim;
}


/* ── /copy picker ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *
 *
 * The same centred card over a dimmed transcript as `/resume` and
 * `/analytics`, because it is the same kind of surface: an overlay the user
 * opened, navigates with a cursor, and leaves with Esc.
 *
 * It differs in pinning its own HEIGHT rather than sizing to content. The
 * widget divides a fixed row budget between the tree and the preview and
 * computes its own `… N more lines` overflow from it, so the card has to be
 * the height the widget measured — an `auto` height would let a long preview
 * grow the card past the screen and clip the footer, which is the only
 * statement of how to leave. `overflow: hidden` for the reason `Screen`
 * carries it: a card taller than its box must not introduce a scrollbar,
 * which silently costs two cells of width and reflows the transcript behind
 * the overlay. */
CopyPickerScreen {
    align: center middle;
    background: $lo-sunken;
}

CopyPickerScreen .copy-picker {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 100;
    height: 90%;
    background: $lo-overlay;
    padding: 1 2;
    overflow: hidden;
}

#copy-picker-body {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    color: $lo-fg;
    overflow: hidden;
}


/* ── /resume session picker ────────────────────────────────────────────── *
 *
 * A floating read surface (overlay ramp, borderless, content-sized, Esc to
 * dismiss) because it answers a "read a list, pick a row" question. It differs
 * from the other read surfaces in being two-way:
 * it owns a cursor and hands an id back to the caller.
 *
 * `max-width` rather than a fixed width so an 80-column terminal gets the
 * card it can hold; the widget measures its own columns against PICKER_WIDTH
 * and truncates the name, which is the one column that can give up cells. */
SessionPickerScreen {
    align: center middle;
    background: $lo-sunken;
}

SessionPickerScreen .session-picker {
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    max-width: 80;
    max-height: 80%;
    background: $lo-overlay;
    padding: 1 2;
}

#session-picker-body {
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    color: $lo-fg;
}


/* ── /move directory picker ────────────────────────────────────────────── *
 *
 * The `/resume` picker's twin, and deliberately identical to it: both answer
 * a "read a list, pick a row" question with a cursor and hand a value back,
 * so giving them different visual language would make one more screen look
 * like a different app.
 *
 * `max-width` rather than a fixed width so an 80-column terminal gets the
 * card it can hold; the widget measures its own columns against
 * PICKER_MAX_WIDTH and truncates the path from the LEFT, which is the one
 * column that can give up cells. Content-sized in both axes like `/resume`,
 * because the row budget is computed in Python (`_page_rows`) and a second
 * cap in the sheet is how a card loses its footer to a clip from the outside. */
MovePickerScreen {
    align: center middle;
    background: $lo-sunken;
}

MovePickerScreen .move-picker {
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    max-width: 86;
    max-height: 80%;
    background: $lo-overlay;
    padding: 1 2;
}

#move-picker-body {
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    color: $lo-fg;
}


/* ── /analytics /usage: the aggregated token-consumption screen ────────── *
 *
 * The same centred card over a dimmed transcript as `/resume`, because it is
 * the same kind of surface — a full-screen overlay the user opened and closes
 * with Esc — and giving diagnostics their own visual language would make one
 * more screen look like a different app. It differs in one structural way:
 * the body can be long (a per-session table grows without bound), so the card
 * pins a title and a hint and puts the report itself in a VerticalScroll
 * between them, rather than sizing to content the way the picker does. */
AnalyticsScreen, SessionScreen {
    align: center middle;
    background: $lo-sunken;
}

AnalyticsScreen .analytics-panel, SessionScreen .analytics-panel {
    /* Responsive: 90% of the terminal, capped generously so a large screen
     * actually gets a larger frame (the token+cost tables have more columns to
     * show) while a small one still fits. The old max-width of 100 left a wide
     * terminal with a narrow card and a sea of dead margin; 140 lets the tables
     * breathe on the big displays this was asked to use, and the 90% keeps a
     * small terminal from overflowing. Height likewise grows to 85% so more
     * rows are visible before scrolling on a tall screen. */
    width: 90%;
    max-width: 140;
    height: 85%;
    max-height: 92%;
    background: $lo-overlay;
    padding: 1 2;
}

#analytics-title, #session-report-title {
    /* Title line + a `─` rule under it (two lines), then a blank padding row
     * before the scrolling body. */
    width: 100%;
    height: 3;
    padding-bottom: 1;
}

#analytics-scroll, #session-report-scroll {
    width: 100%;
    height: 1fr;
    /* A grabbable scrollbar consistent with the transcript's: `edge` keeps it
     * structurally quiet when idle, hover lights it to `muted` so a pointer
     * finds a target to drag. `scrollbar-gutter: stable` reserves the column so
     * the report's right-aligned number columns do not shift when the thumb
     * appears. Textual's own scrollbar is draggable, so styling it IS the
     * "grab and drag to scroll" affordance. */
    scrollbar-size-vertical: 1;
    scrollbar-gutter: stable;
    scrollbar-background: $lo-sunken;
    /* `muted` idle (not `edge`) so this bar reads as the SAME chrome as the
     * hand-painted `/usage` thumb, whose idle glyph is `muted` (design D1/D2):
     * an `edge`-idle bar was near-invisible next to the bright `/usage` one, so
     * the two overlays spoke two scrollbar languages and scroll was hard to
     * discover here. Hover/active still step up so the grab target is obvious. */
    scrollbar-color: $lo-muted;
    scrollbar-color-hover: $lo-muted;
    scrollbar-color-active: $lo-accent;
}

#analytics-body, #session-report-body {
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    color: $lo-fg;
}

#analytics-hint, #session-report-hint {
    width: 100%;
    height: 2;
    padding-top: 1;
    color: $lo-faint;
}


/* Unlike the all-session aligned tables, session diagnostics wrap long IDs
 * and model names. Width must be constrained to the scroll viewport so a
 * 50-column terminal never acquires a horizontal scrollbar. */
#session-report-body {
    width: 100%;
}

#session-report-title {
    text-style: bold;
}

#session-report-hint {
    color: $lo-muted;
}

/* ── prompts: the ask picker and the approval gate ────────────────────── *
 *
 * The same "read a list, pick a row" card as `/resume` above, and deliberately
 * not a variation on it: giving the agent's own question a different visual
 * language would make the one surface the user did not open look like a
 * different application.
 *
 * Where it DOES differ is placement, and that is the whole point of these
 * rules. `/resume` is a modal the user opened and can close; these two are
 * questions the turn is parked on, raised by the agent while the user was
 * reading something else. As modals they covered the conversation they were
 * asking about — the tool output, the error, the plan — so answering meant
 * dismissing the question to go and look, and scrollback was unreachable.
 * Anchored in the dock they are a band above the composer instead: the
 * transcript keeps the rest of the screen and all of its scrollback, and the
 * question stays put while the user scrolls back for what they need.
 *
 * ── the prompt host ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── *
 *
 * A positioner, like `#band` and for the same reasons: it reserves rows only
 * while it holds a question, so an ordinary session pays nothing for it, and
 * it carries the dock's fill so the rows it does reserve read as part of the
 * input rather than as a slab of transcript.
 *
 * It sits at the TOP of `#input-dock`, above `#band`. That ordering is the
 * feature: the band is status (subagent jobs, todos) and a prompt is a
 * blocker, so the thing being waited on stays closest to the composer, and a
 * status list that grows and shrinks as jobs come and go cannot shift the
 * question under the user's cursor while they are answering it.
 *
 * `display` is toggled in Python (`OperatorApp._mount_prompt`) rather than
 * driven by `:empty`, which Textual does not implement: an empty container
 * with padding still paints its padding rows, and this one must be exactly
 * zero rows when nothing is being asked.
 *
 * The one blank row BELOW the prompt lives HERE, on the host, and not on the
 * card. That is a correctness constraint rather than a preference: the card is
 * itself the slot (the app mounts it directly into this host), so a
 * `.prompt-slot` padding rule and the card's own `padding` rule would target
 * ONE widget and conflict — and Textual resolves that by specificity, where
 * the class selector beats the type selector. Written that way the card
 * silently lost its vertical padding to the slot's rule, `CARD_PADDING_ROWS`
 * no longer described the real geometry, and the dock came out taller than the
 * screen (measured at 80x12: `virtual_size` 12 against `size` 10, the
 * scrollable screen AGENTS.md calls always a bug here). One owner per
 * property: the host owns the separation row, the card owns its padding. */
#prompt-host {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
    background: $lo-surface;
    display: none;
    padding: 0 0 1 0;
}

/* The card itself. No longer a modal backdrop: this is a raised panel in the
 * dock, so it gets the elevation step (`$lo-overlay` over the dock's
 * `$lo-surface`) that this kit uses everywhere instead of a border.
 *
 * `width: 1fr` rather than `auto`, which is a change from the modal version
 * and is load-bearing: an auto-width card sizes to its widest LINE, so the
 * question's own wrapping decided how wide the panel was and the card visibly
 * changed width as the user moved between questions of a multi-question ask.
 * Full-width in the dock, the panel is stable — and this rule is now the ONLY
 * statement of how wide the question is. `AskPickerScreen._card_width` reads
 * this column back rather than budgeting its own against the terminal: it used
 * to cap the text at 74 cells, a modal-era figure that survived the move into
 * the dock, so the ink stopped a third of the way short of the panel's own fill
 * on a wide terminal while the composer directly beneath it ran the full width.
 * One owner for the width, and it is this line. */
AskPickerScreen {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
    background: $lo-overlay;
    padding: 1 1;
}

/* Focus is not decorated. The card takes focus the moment it mounts and holds
 * it until it is answered, so a focus ring would be permanent furniture — and
 * the one state worth showing is the opposite one (focus LOST to the
 * composer), which the app fixes by handing focus back rather than by
 * painting it. */
/* The card's inner body holder. Height stays `auto` and is NOT capped here:
 * the card budgets its own rows in Python (`AskPickerScreen._body_rows`,
 * which reserves the transcript's share and the rest of the dock), and a
 * second cap in the stylesheet is how this card lost its footer once already
 * — the widget laid out the rows its own budget allowed, the sheet clipped to
 * a different number from the outside, and the difference came off the tail,
 * taking the keys and the only statement of how to leave with it (D1).
 * One budget, in one place, and the sheet does not second-guess it. */
AskPickerScreen .ask-picker {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
    padding: 0;
}

#ask-picker-body {
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    color: $lo-fg;
}
